# Recipe 35: Authentic Israeli Chopped Salad
updates[35] = {
    "directions": [
        "The key to this salad is the cut, and everything else is secondary. Every single piece of cucumber and tomato must be diced to a uniform, very small size, roughly 1/4-inch cubes. This is not a rough chop or a casual dice. It is a precision exercise that requires a sharp chef's knife and your full attention. The uniform size ensures that every single bite contains a perfect, balanced ratio of cucumber to tomato, that the dressing coats every piece evenly, and that the salad has the distinctive fine-grained texture that Israelis would recognize as authentic. A rough, uneven chop produces a different salad entirely.",
        "Using a sharp chef's knife, dice the cucumbers into precise 1/4-inch pieces. If using regular cucumbers, peel them first and cut them in half lengthwise, then scoop out the watery seeds with a small spoon before dicing, as the seeds release excess liquid that dilutes the salad. Persian or mini cucumbers can be used as-is without peeling or seeding, as their seeds are small and their skin is thin and not bitter. Cut the cucumbers into planks lengthwise, then into strips, then crosswise into cubes. Consistency of size matters more than speed.",
        "Dice the tomatoes to the same 1/4-inch size as the cucumbers. Selecting the right tomatoes is important: use firm, ripe specimens that yield only slightly when squeezed. Tomatoes that are too soft will turn to mush when diced this small and release a flood of juice that turns the salad into soup within minutes. Roma tomatoes or firm-ripe beefsteak tomatoes work well. Cut each tomato in half, gently squeeze out and discard the excess seeds and jelly, then dice the flesh into uniform cubes. Wipe down your cutting board between the tomatoes and the next ingredient, as the accumulated tomato juice will make everything else slippery.",
        "Mince the red onion very finely, aiming for pieces that are noticeably smaller than the cucumber and tomato cubes, nearly invisible. The onion provides a sharp, pungent background note that should be present in every bite without dominating. If the onion pieces are too large, they will overwhelm the delicate balance of the salad and leave an unpleasant raw bite that lingers. If you find raw onion too sharp, soak the minced onion in ice water for 5 minutes, then drain thoroughly, which tames the harsh sulfur compounds while preserving the flavor.",
        "Combine the diced cucumbers, diced tomatoes, and minced red onion in a large glass or ceramic bowl. Glass is preferable because it does not absorb flavors or react with the acidic dressing. Add the finely chopped parsley and mint, distributing the herbs evenly throughout the vegetables. Toss gently with your hands or two spoons to mix without crushing the vegetables.",
        "Prepare the dressing just before serving, and dress the salad at the very last possible moment. This timing instruction is not a suggestion but the single most important rule of Israeli chopped salad. In a small bowl, whisk together the fresh lemon juice and extra virgin olive oil until combined. Pour the dressing over the salad, season generously with salt and freshly ground black pepper, and toss gently but thoroughly to coat every piece evenly.",
        "Serve immediately, within minutes of dressing. Israeli chopped salad does not hold, does not wait, and does not improve with time. The salt in the dressing begins pulling water out of the cucumbers and tomatoes within minutes, and after 15 to 20 minutes the salad becomes a watery, limp, soggy pool that bears no resemblance to the crispy, vibrant dish you just assembled. Make it last, dress it last, and eat it first. This salad is the opening act of the meal, never an afterthought."
    ],
    "tip": "Dress this salad at the very last possible moment before serving, because the salt pulls water out of the cucumbers and tomatoes within minutes, turning a crispy, vibrant salad into a watery puddle. Use the firmest tomatoes you can find and do not skip removing the seeds, which contribute excess moisture. This salad belongs at the start of every Israeli meal and pairs with virtually everything. For a heartier version, toss in diced avocado and crumbled feta cheese just before serving, which transforms it from a side salad into a substantial first course."
}

# Recipe 36: Zaalouk
